      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C249","C284","C292","C250","C290","C257","C260"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct eravacycline-specific absolute oral bioavailability, permeability, efflux, or intestinal-metabolism record is present in the supplied cards; the decision relies on transfer from omadacycline and tetracycline.",
    "The target species is not explicitly specified; omadacycline lacks oral bioavailability in rodents (C108) and tetracycline rat in situ absorption is low (C220), so a rodent-based endpoint would substantially weaken the high prediction.",
    "Formulation and prandial conditions for the query are unspecified; omadacycline F ranges from 0.5% for a slow-dissolution tablet to 34.5% for a standard fasted tablet (C49, C18).",
    "Potential eravacycline-specific P-gp efflux and intestinal metabolism are not directly measured; L5 tetracycline P-gp/mucin records are class-level only.",
    "Most L5 records are for unrelated molecules (fluoroquinolones, anthracyclines, nintedanib, danoprevir, etc.) and were not used as evidence for the query."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F≥20%) because the query is a fluorocycline tetracycline and the closest supplied empirical contexts—omadacycline (34.5% human, 31.2% CF, 31% monkey) and tetracycline (75% human)—show oral bioavailability above threshold; L3 oral exposure records for tetracycline (Cmax 4.57 µg/mL, AUC0-∞ 67.4 µg·h/mL at 500 mg) reinforce that the tetracycline class is orally absorbed; L5 adds mixed class-level gut-wall evidence (tetracycline P-gp/mucin barriers vs doxycycline good permeability and low efflux) that does not overturn the human class-level evidence; the low physicochemical prior is not affirmative evidence of F<20% and no new contradictory eravacycline-specific evidence was supplied."
}

